<BaseLayout title="Two Road Trips on a Brand-New License — Huanyu Chen"
  description="May 2022: a new driver's license and two road trips in one month. A loop through Bay Area campuses, Silicon Valley and Highway 1, then a late-May snowstorm in Yellowstone and the Tetons.">
  <p class="muted"><a href="/misc">← Misc</a> · May 2022</p>
  <h1>Two Road Trips on a Brand-New License</h1>
  <p class="muted trip-sub">Bay Area campuses and Highway 1, then snow in Yellowstone and the Tetons, all in one May.</p>

  <div class="callout trip-stats">
    <strong>Two trips · May 17–25 and May 28–31, 2022</strong><br />
    Part one: Los Angeles → Berkeley → San Francisco → Stanford → Silicon Valley → Capitola → Highway 1 →
    Santa Barbara → Los Angeles<br />
    Part two: Los Angeles ✈ Salt Lake City → West Yellowstone → Yellowstone → Grand Teton → Jackson → Salt Lake City<br />
    two groups of friends · one new driver at the wheel · four states · two national parks · snow on Memorial Day
  </div>

  <p>
    On May 11, 2022, I got my driver's license. A license is a small plastic card, but it changes what a map
    is. Places that used to need someone else's car, or a bus schedule, or a flight, become places you can simply
    drive to. In Southern California, where nearly everything is a drive away, that is a very big change.
  </p>
  <p>
    I was, to put it mildly, excited about driving. Six days after I got my license, I was on the road, and I did
    two road trips that month, each with a different group of friends, with me doing the driving on both. The first was a rental-car loop from Los Angeles up
    through the Central Valley to the Bay Area. We spent a week among campuses, cable cars and tech headquarters, then
    came home down the coast on Highway 1. The second, three days later, started with a flight to Salt Lake City and a
    long drive north into Yellowstone. There a late-May snowstorm was waiting for us.
  </p>
  <p>
    As with my other trip posts, everything below is reconstructed from the camera roll: the phone's timestamps and
    locations, lined up in order. Where the photos are silent (there are none at all from May 26 and 27, the two
    days between the trips), I've left the gap rather than fill it with a guess.
  </p>

  <h2>Part one: Bay Area campuses and the coast</h2>

  <figure class="trip-fig wide-fig">
    <a href={P + 'map-loop.jpg'}><img src={P + 'map-loop.jpg'} alt="Map of the first trip: Los Angeles north through the Central Valley to Berkeley, San Francisco, Stanford and Silicon Valley, then south via Capitola, Highway 1 and Santa Barbara back to Los Angeles." loading="lazy" /></a>
    <figcaption>Part one: eight days, up the inland freeway and home along the ocean.</figcaption>
  </figure>

  <h3>Up the Central Valley</h3>
  <p>
    The car was a gray rental sedan, picked up in Los Angeles at a quarter past ten on the morning of Tuesday, May 17.
    The camera roll opens with the slow, careful walk-around that every new renter learns to do: every scratch and
    dent photographed, the odometer, the trunk. Then the road. The quickest way from Los Angeles to the Bay Area is
    Interstate 5, which runs almost ruler-straight up the west side of the Central Valley for some two hundred miles.
  </p>
  <p>
    It is not a scenic road in the postcard sense. It is something better for a new driver: long, flat, wide and
    honest, with the Coast Ranges a faint brown line to the west and nothing but orchards and fields to the east. The
    valley grows a huge share of America's fruit, nuts and vegetables, and from the freeway you see it as endless
    rows of almond and pistachio trees flicking past like the teeth of a comb. By half past six we were in Merced
    County with the evening sun still high, and by nine we had reached the East Bay and stopped for the night in
    Richmond.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'central-valley.jpg'} alt="View from a car window on Interstate 5 across flat farmland and orchards under a clear blue sky." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>I-5 through Merced County, the first evening.</figcaption></figure>

  <h3>Berkeley: Bear Territory</h3>
  <figure class="trip-fig wide-fig">
    <a href={P + 'daymap-0518.jpg'}><img src={P + 'daymap-0518.jpg'} alt="Map of May 18: a walk across the UC Berkeley campus, the Berkeley Marina, then Battery Cranston by the Golden Gate Bridge." loading="lazy" /></a>
    <figcaption>Day 2: Berkeley in the morning, the marina in the afternoon, the Golden Gate at sunset.</figcaption>
  </figure>
  <p>
    The next morning we parked by the football stadium and walked onto the campus of the <strong>University of
    California, Berkeley</strong>. The first thing to greet us was a long blue-and-gold banner over the stadium steps:
    "Welcome to Bear Territory". Cal's mascot is the golden bear, and the campus takes it seriously. Just around the
    corner, under a great ivy-covered arch, is the courtyard of the Haas School of Business, as green and shaded as a
    cloister. For a visitor from USC, the banner reads like a friendly warning.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'berkeley-bear-territory.jpg'} alt="Wide stone steps leading up to California Memorial Stadium under a blue and gold Welcome to Bear Territory banner." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The stadium steps, and fair warning.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'berkeley-haas-arch.jpg'} alt="A tall arch completely covered in ivy, framing the courtyard of the Haas School of Business." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The ivy arch at the Haas School of Business.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    You can see Berkeley's landmark from almost anywhere on campus. <strong>Sather Tower</strong>, universally called
    the Campanile, is a 307-foot granite bell tower finished in 1914, modeled loosely on the campanile in St Mark's
    Square in Venice. It holds a carillon of sixty-one bells that is played by hand several times a day, and its upper
    floors famously store fossils from the university's paleontology collection, dinosaur bones and all, because the
    tower is cool, dry and very hard to break into. Beside it stands <strong>Doe Library</strong>, a long Beaux-Arts
    palace of white granite completed in 1911, with a row of tall arched windows along its north reading room.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'berkeley-campanile.jpg'} alt="Sather Tower, a tall white granite bell tower, rising above trees against a deep blue sky." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Sather Tower, the Campanile.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'berkeley-doe-library.jpg'} alt="The long neoclassical granite facade of Doe Library with tall windows above a lawn." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Doe Library.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    The walk ended at the south edge of campus at <strong>Sather Gate</strong>, a 1910 bronze-and-granite arch that for
    decades was the official boundary of the university. Students handed out flyers and made speeches just outside it,
    on city land, where campus rules didn't apply. In 1964 that strip of sidewalk and the plaza in front of
    <strong>Sproul Hall</strong> became the stage of the Free Speech Movement, the sit-ins and speeches that set the tone
    for a decade of student protest across the country. In the pavement nearby, a bronze medallion marks the Springer
    Memorial Gateway, the gift of a member of the class of 1902.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'berkeley-sather-gate.jpg'} alt="Sather Gate, an ornate green bronze arch between granite pillars, with students walking through." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Sather Gate (1910).</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'berkeley-gateway-seal.jpg'} alt="A bronze medallion reading Springer Memorial Gateway set into patterned stone paving." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The Springer Memorial Gateway medallion.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    In the student store off Sproul Plaza, a plastic bin by the register held a pile of pin-back buttons in Cal's blue
    and gold. "BEAT ASU." "BEAT UCLA." And, sitting right on top as if it had been waiting for me, "BEAT USC". There
    is no rivalry quite like the one between two branches of the same family, and Berkeley and UCLA have been at it for
    a century; USC, the private school across town from UCLA, is on the list too. The buttons got photographed.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'berkeley-beat-usc.jpg'} alt="A yellow and blue button reading BEAT USC lying in a clear plastic bin." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Hello to you too.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'berkeley-beat-ucla.jpg'} alt="A bin of yellow and blue buttons reading BEAT ASU and BEAT UCLA." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Equal-opportunity buttons.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    After lunch downtown (a bowl of beef pho, then gelato), we walked back up to the northeast corner of campus for
    the <strong>College of Chemistry</strong>. For a chemistry nerd, Berkeley is holy ground. Glenn Seaborg and his
    colleagues here discovered or co-discovered a long run of elements, from plutonium onward, and two of them are
    named for the place itself: berkelium (element 97) and californium (98). By that night a navy hoodie had come home
    with us, printed like a square from the periodic table: <em>Bk, Berkelium, 97</em>.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig narrow-fig"><img src={P + 'berkeley-chemistry.jpg'} alt="A tall modern building with a pagoda-like roofline at the College of Chemistry on the Berkeley campus." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The College of Chemistry, home of element 97.</figcaption></figure>

  <h3>The Berkeley Marina</h3>
  <p>
    In the afternoon we drove down to the water. The <strong>Berkeley Marina</strong> sits at the end of University
    Avenue on land reclaimed from the bay, and from the shoreline park you get one of the best views in the region: the
    whole San Francisco skyline laid out across the water, with Salesforce Tower poking up in the middle and the Bay
    Bridge running off to the left. The afternoon was clear and the bay was a hard, glittering blue.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'berkeley-sf-skyline.jpg'} alt="The San Francisco skyline seen across the bay from Berkeley, hazy blue in the afternoon light." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>San Francisco across the bay, from the Berkeley shoreline.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    The rocks along the shore had residents. A California ground squirrel stood up against a sun-warmed boulder, completely uninterested in us,
    gazing out at the bay like a retired sailor. A few minutes later one popped up between
    the rocks with something in its paws, ate it in a hurry, and vanished again. We ate too: a takeout box of rice
    noodle rolls, eaten in the car by the water, with the marina's forest of masts bobbing just beyond the windshield.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'berkeley-marina-cat.jpg'} alt="A California ground squirrel standing up against a large sunlit rock by the shore, seen from behind." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>A local, contemplating the bay.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'berkeley-ground-squirrel.jpg'} alt="A California ground squirrel eating between rocks and dry grass." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Another local, busier.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>

  <h3>The Golden Gate at golden hour</h3>
  <p>
    Then across the Bay Bridge and through the city to the Presidio, the old army post at the northern tip of San
    Francisco. Just after half past six we were on the Coastal Trail at <strong>Battery Cranston</strong>, one of the
    concrete gun emplacements built in the 1890s to guard the strait. The guns are long gone, and what's left is one of
    the finest viewpoints in the city. The <strong>Golden Gate Bridge</strong> fills the frame, close enough that you
    can make out the rivets, with the green-gold Marin Headlands rising on the other side.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'golden-gate-cranston.jpg'} alt="The south tower of the Golden Gate Bridge rising above cypress trees and the Presidio, with the Marin Headlands behind." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The bridge from Battery Cranston, 6:48 pm.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'golden-gate-evening.jpg'} alt="The Golden Gate Bridge and the strait in evening light, with green slopes in the foreground." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Twenty minutes later, as the light turned.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    The bridge opened in 1937, and for almost three decades its main span was the longest in the world. Its color
    was never supposed to be permanent. "International orange" was the primer, and the consulting architect, Irving
    Morrow, argued to keep it because it stood out in the fog and matched the warm hills. It looks different every
    few minutes. In flat noon light it's brick red. At seven in the evening, with the sun low over the Pacific, it
    goes the color of embers.
  </p>
  <p>
    Turn around and you're looking west, straight out through the Golden Gate to the open ocean. The sun was setting
    into a hazy sky over the water, laying a long silver path across the waves toward the cliffs. Walk a little further
    along the trail and the coast opens up southward, the bluffs dropping steeply to the surf at Baker Beach, with the
    houses of Sea Cliff stacked along the ridge.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'pacific-sun.jpg'} alt="The low sun over the Pacific, casting a bright silver path on the water beyond dark cliffs." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Looking out the Golden Gate to the Pacific.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'sea-cliff.jpg'} alt="Steep green bluffs dropping to rocky surf, with the Sea Cliff neighborhood on the ridge beyond." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The bluffs toward Baker Beach and Sea Cliff.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    Dinner, just before nine, was in the Richmond District, the foggy western neighborhood that has become one of
    San Francisco's two Chinatowns. We had lamb skewers crusted with cumin and chili, spicy dumplings, a bubbling tray
    of fish and tofu, and grilled oysters under a blanket of garlic and scallion. Then down the Peninsula to San Mateo
    for the night.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig narrow-fig"><img src={P + 'lamb-skewers.jpg'} alt="A row of cumin-crusted lamb skewers on paper on a dark tray." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Lamb skewers in the Richmond District.</figcaption></figure>

  <h3>San Francisco by cable car</h3>
  <figure class="trip-fig wide-fig">
    <a href={P + 'daymap-0519.jpg'}><img src={P + 'daymap-0519.jpg'} alt="Map of May 19 in San Francisco: Pier 43, a cable car to Powell and Market, Union Square, Salesforce Tower, the Ferry Building, California Street, Lombard Street, Pier 39 and Twin Peaks." loading="lazy" /></a>
    <figcaption>Day 3: San Francisco, almost entirely on rails and on foot.</figcaption>
  </figure>
  <p>
    The next day was San Francisco, done properly. We started at Fisherman's Wharf just after ten. The white
    <strong>Pier 43 arch</strong> stands at the water's edge with Alcatraz framed in the bay behind it. It is a
    relic of the old ferry slips, from the days when the railroads ran freight cars onto barges here, and it is now
    a very good place to stand and look at Alcatraz.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'pier43-arch.jpg'} alt="The white arched Pier 43 ferry arch on a wooden wharf, with the bay and Alcatraz behind." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The Pier 43 arch at Fisherman's Wharf.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    A few blocks inland, at Taylor and Bay, cable car number 22 was waiting at the end of the Powell–Mason line. San
    Francisco's cable cars are the last manually operated system of their kind in the world. There's no motor on the
    car at all: a steel cable runs continuously in a slot under the street, pulled by engines in a powerhouse on Mason
    Street, and the gripman on board clamps onto it to go and lets go to stop. You can hear it humming under the road
    the whole way.
  </p>
  <p>
    Riding one up and over Nob Hill is exactly as good as the postcards promise. The car tilts, the bell clangs, and
    through the open frame of the windows the city appears in steep slices: a canyon of Victorian bay windows with the
    Transamerica Pyramid at the end of it, then the crossing with California Street and its own cable tracks running
    down to the bay.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'cable-car-22.jpg'} alt="Cable car number 22, wooden with red, cream and gold trim, at the Taylor and Bay terminus." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Car 22, Powell–Mason line.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'cable-car-transamerica.jpg'} alt="The Transamerica Pyramid seen down a steep street, framed by the window of a cable car." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The Transamerica Pyramid, through the cable car's window.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    The line ends at Powell and Market, where the cars are turned around by hand on a wooden turntable, and where a
    stone plaque on Hallidie Plaza honors the man who started it all. Andrew Hallidie was a wire-rope manufacturer who,
    the story goes, watched a team of horses slip on a wet hill and get dragged down it with their streetcar. In 1873
    he ran the first cable car up Clay Street. A few blocks north is <strong>Union Square</strong>, with the
    Dewey Monument rising out of the middle: a tall column topped by a bronze Victory, put up in 1903 to celebrate the
    US Navy's win at Manila Bay.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'hallidie-plaque.jpg'} alt="A weathered stone plaque dedicated to Andrew S. Hallidie, 1836 to 1900, with a metal cable-car grip mounted on it." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Andrew Hallidie's plaque, with a cable-car grip.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'union-square.jpg'} alt="Union Square with the tall Dewey Monument column and historic buildings behind under a blue sky." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Union Square and the Dewey Monument.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    Lunch was hot pot a block off Geary: a split pot, one side mild and milky, the other dark with spices, and platters
    of thinly rolled beef and lamb. Afterwards we walked east through the Financial District. In the space of fifteen
    minutes the city goes from the vaulted glass roof of the Crocker Galleria to the new towers south of Market. The
    biggest is <strong>Salesforce Tower</strong>, finished in 2018 and, at 1,070 feet, the tallest building in San
    Francisco. Craning your neck at the bottom of it, the glass curves up and away like the hull of a ship.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'hot-pot-geary.jpg'} alt="A split hot pot with a pale and a spicy broth, plates of rolled sliced beef and lamb, and vegetables." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Hot pot for lunch.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'salesforce-tower.jpg'} alt="Looking straight up the curved glass side of Salesforce Tower against a blue sky." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Salesforce Tower, from the bottom.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    At the foot of Market Street stands the <strong>Ferry Building</strong>, finished in 1898 with a clock tower
    modeled on the Giralda in Seville. Before the bridges opened in the 1930s, nearly everyone arriving in San
    Francisco from the east came through it, tens of thousands of commuters a day. For decades afterwards it was
    walled off from the city by an elevated freeway. The 1989 earthquake damaged the freeway, it was torn down, and the
    waterfront got its front door back.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'ferry-building.jpg'} alt="The Ferry Building with its white clock tower, framed by palm trees, with people in the plaza in front." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The Ferry Building, framed by palms.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    Then a second cable car, this time the California Street line, which climbs straight up over Nob Hill through the
    edge of Chinatown. From the back of the car you look down the hill at pagoda-roofed buildings with their painted
    balconies, red and green against the towers of the Financial District, and the tracks running straight down to
    the bay. Old St. Mary's, the city's first cathedral, slides past on the corner.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig narrow-fig"><img src={P + 'chinatown-cable-car.jpg'} alt="Looking down California Street from a cable car, past pagoda-roofed Chinatown buildings toward downtown towers." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Down California Street through Chinatown.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    The afternoon's obligatory stop was <strong>Lombard Street</strong>, the "crookedest street in the world", with
    eight hairpin turns packed into one steep block of Russian Hill. It was built that way in 1922 because the hill was
    too steep for cars to go straight down. Today its brick switchbacks are lined with hydrangeas and lovingly kept
    flower beds, and the residents share them with a steady procession of tourists. From the top, the view runs
    over the rooftops of North Beach to Coit Tower and the bay.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'lombard-coit.jpg'} alt="View from the top of Lombard Street over flowerbeds and rooftops to Coit Tower and the bay." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>From the top of Lombard Street, toward Coit Tower.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    Back at the wharf, ice cream came first, then the main event. Since late 1989, a few months after the Loma Prieta
    earthquake, California sea lions have taken over the floating docks at <strong>Pier 39</strong>. Nobody quite knows
    why they chose it, but the marina gave up and let them have it. On a sunny afternoon they lie in great piles on the
    docks, barking, shoving and occasionally flopping into the water, a few hundred feet from a souvenir shop.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'sea-lions.jpg'} alt="Sea lions lying in heaps on the floating wooden docks at Pier 39, with the city behind." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The sea lions of Pier 39.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    We ended the day the way you should end a day in San Francisco: on top of it. <strong>Twin Peaks</strong> rises
    about 900 feet near the geographic center of the city, and from the overlook at half past six the whole place is
    spread out at your feet. The grid of Market Street runs straight to the bay, downtown bristles in the middle, and
    the Bay Bridge and the East Bay hills sit beyond. From up here you can trace the entire day.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'twin-peaks.jpg'} alt="Panorama of downtown San Francisco and the bay from the Twin Peaks overlook in early evening." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The whole city from Twin Peaks, 6:25 pm.</figcaption></figure>

  <h3>Golden Gate Park from the top of a wheel</h3>
  <figure class="trip-fig wide-fig">
    <a href={P + 'daymap-0520.jpg'}><img src={P + 'daymap-0520.jpg'} alt="Map of May 20 and 21: Golden Gate Park in San Francisco, then down the Peninsula to Stanford and the Dumbarton Bridge." loading="lazy" /></a>
    <figcaption>Days 4–5: Golden Gate Park, then two days at Stanford.</figcaption>
  </figure>
  <p>
    Friday started in <strong>Golden Gate Park</strong>, at the Music Concourse, the sunken plaza between the park's two
    big museums. Towering over it was the SkyStar observation wheel, put up in 2020 for the park's 150th anniversary
    and meant to be temporary. On one side of the concourse is the
    <strong>California Academy of Sciences</strong>, and we went there first.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'skystar-wheel.jpg'} alt="The white SkyStar Ferris wheel above the trees at the Music Concourse in Golden Gate Park, under a cloudless sky." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The SkyStar wheel over the Music Concourse.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    The Academy's lobby is ruled by a cast of a <em>Tyrannosaurus rex</em> skeleton, mid-stride under the glass roof.
    The building's centerpiece is a four-story rainforest under a glass dome, warm and humid, with a spiral ramp
    climbing up through the canopy. Two blue-and-gold macaws sat on a branch near the top, splendidly unbothered, and
    a black-and-red butterfly landed on the boardwalk at our feet.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'cal-academy-trex.jpg'} alt="A T. rex skeleton cast in the bright glass-roofed lobby of the California Academy of Sciences." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The T. rex in the Academy's lobby.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'cal-academy-macaws.jpg'} alt="Two blue-and-gold macaws perched on branches inside the rainforest dome." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Macaws in the rainforest dome.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    After lunch we rode the wheel. From the top of SkyStar, the concourse turns into a diagram. Directly below is the
    Francis Scott Key monument of 1888. Its statue of Key was pulled down in 2020, and since 2021 the monument has been
    ringed by Dana King's <em>Monumental Reckoning</em>: 350 dark sculpted figures, one for each of the first enslaved
    Africans brought to Virginia in 1619. Beyond it the Academy's roof reveals its secret: it is
    alive, two and a half acres of native plants on rolling green humps, designed by Renzo Piano to keep the building
    cool. Across the concourse, the de Young Museum's twisted copper tower rises out of the trees. On the skyline,
    Sutro Tower stands over everything like a giant red-and-white trident.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'skystar-key-monument.jpg'} alt="Aerial view from the wheel of the Francis Scott Key monument ringed by black figures, with the tree-lined concourse beyond." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The Francis Scott Key monument from above.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'skystar-living-roof.jpg'} alt="The green living roof of the California Academy of Sciences seen from above, with Sutro Tower on the hill behind." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The Academy's living roof, and Sutro Tower.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>

  <h3>Stanford</h3>
  <p>
    That afternoon we drove down the Peninsula to Redwood City. After bubble tea and a Japanese set dinner in Palo
    Alto, we got to <strong>Stanford</strong> at dusk. The
    first stop was a strange and lovely one: the <strong>Arizona Cactus Garden</strong>, laid out in the early 1880s
    for Jane and Leland Stanford beside what was supposed to become their mansion. The mansion was never built. The
    garden survived, half-forgotten for decades, and was restored in the 1990s. In the blue light after sunset it's a
    landscape from another planet: columnar cacti, spiky agaves and fat barrel cacti along sandy paths, with the
    oaks of the Arboretum all around.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'stanford-cactus-garden.jpg'} alt="Tall cacti, agaves and spiky desert plants along a sandy path in the Arizona Cactus Garden at dusk." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The Arizona Cactus Garden at dusk.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    Saturday was the full campus day. Near the bookstore an A-frame sign announced the weekend's big series: No. 4
    Stanford against USC at Sunken Diamond, Thursday, Friday and Saturday at noon, students free. It seems USC was
    following me up the state. Then came the landmarks. <strong>Hoover Tower</strong> is 285 feet of sandstone-colored
    concrete with a red-tiled dome, built in 1941 for the university's fiftieth anniversary to house Herbert Hoover's
    huge collection of documents on war and revolution.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'stanford-usc-baseball.jpg'} alt="An A-frame sign reading No. 4 Baseball, Stanford vs USC at Sunken Diamond, with game times for May 19, 20 and 21." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>USC, again.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'hoover-tower.jpg'} alt="Hoover Tower rising against a bright blue sky, seen from below past green trees." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Hoover Tower (1941).</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    The heart of the campus is the <strong>Main Quad</strong>. Frederick Law Olmsted, of Central Park fame, did the
    site plan, and the buildings, by the Boston firm of Shepley, Rutan and Coolidge, mix Romanesque arches with Spanish
    mission red tile. The result is the most recognizable campus look in the West: long arcades of rough-cut buff
    sandstone, deep shade under the arches, and dazzling sun on the courtyard. Seen from the side, Hoover Tower rises
    behind the quad like a bell tower that wandered over from somewhere else.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'stanford-quad-hoover.jpg'} alt="A sandstone building with arched windows beside a fountain and flowerbeds, with Hoover Tower behind." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Hoover Tower over the edge of the quad.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'stanford-arcade.jpg'} alt="A long sandstone arcade of Romanesque arches in the Main Quad, with sunlight on the floor." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The arcades of the Main Quad.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    After lunch in a campus dining hall, we went to an afternoon concert at <strong>Bing Concert Hall</strong>: the
    Stanford Philharmonia's spring concert. Bing is Stanford's 2013 concert hall, a "vineyard" design in which terraced
    seating wraps all the way around the stage, with billowing sail-like panels overhead to shape the sound.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'bing-concert-hall.jpg'} alt="An orchestra seated on the stage of Bing Concert Hall, surrounded by terraced wooden seating under curving panels." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The Stanford Philharmonia on stage at Bing Concert Hall.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    The day ended, appropriately for a computer graphics student, at the Engineering Quad. The <strong>Gates Computer
    Science Building</strong> wears its sandstone like the rest of the campus. It is a modern building in the old
    style, its name carved over a great arched window, and it holds one of the most famous computer science
    departments in the world. Google's founders were graduate students in this building in the 1990s when they wrote
    the first version of their search engine.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig narrow-fig"><img src={P + 'stanford-gates.jpg'} alt="The entrance of the Gates Computer Science building, with a large arched window in rough-cut sandstone." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The Gates Computer Science Building.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    At seven in the evening we drove out to the bay shore at the <strong>Dumbarton Bridge</strong>, where the Bayfront
    Expressway runs along the edge of the Don Edwards San Francisco Bay National Wildlife Refuge. It is a flat,
    quiet, enormous landscape of tidal marsh and old salt-evaporation ponds. The low sun lit up the dry grass, the
    long bridge ran off toward the East Bay hills, and a line of power pylons marched across the water. Silicon Valley
    is ten minutes away, and you would never know it. That night we stayed at a hotel near Stanford.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'dumbarton-bridge.jpg'} alt="The long, low Dumbarton Bridge stretching across the bay beyond a grassy marsh in golden evening light." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The Dumbarton Bridge at sunset.</figcaption></figure>

  <h3>Silicon Valley, one logo at a time</h3>
  <figure class="trip-fig wide-fig">
    <a href={P + 'daymap-0522.jpg'}><img src={P + 'daymap-0522.jpg'} alt="Map of May 22 and 23: the Computer History Museum and Castro Street in Mountain View, then Apple Park, Intel, Google and Oracle." loading="lazy" /></a>
    <figcaption>Days 6–7: museum, hot pot, and a pilgrimage to four headquarters.</figcaption>
  </figure>
  <p>
    Sunday began at the <strong>Computer History Museum</strong> in Mountain View, which holds the largest collection
    of computing artifacts in the world. Its newest permanent exhibit, <em>Make Software: Change the World!</em>, tells
    the story through the software itself rather than the machines: the programs behind everything from MP3s and
    photo editing to car crash simulations and Wikipedia. One wall is a long, bright collage of a city street at night,
    every screen and sign in it running on code.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'chm-make-software.jpg'} alt="The entrance panel of the Make Software: Change the World exhibit inside the Computer History Museum." loading="lazy" /><figcaption><em>Make Software: Change the World!</em> at the Computer History Museum.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    We had lunch in the museum café. In the afternoon we drove down to San Jose's Little Saigon and spent a couple of
    hours singing in a karaoke room, then came back up to Mountain View.
  </p>
  <p>
    That evening's dinner on Castro Street in Mountain View deserves its own photo. Chongqing-style
    <strong>nine-grid hot pot</strong> divides one big pot of chili-red broth into nine square compartments with a
    wooden grid. The idea is that the center square, sitting right over the flame, is hottest, the corners are coolest,
    and you choose a square for each ingredient depending on how long it should cook. Around it went platters of
    sliced beef and lamb, vegetables, and in the middle of the table a fat golden koi carved out of fried dough.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'nine-grid-hot-pot.jpg'} alt="A table seen from above: a round pot divided into nine square compartments of red broth, surrounded by small plates of meat, vegetables and sauces on a red table." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Nine-grid hot pot in Mountain View.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    Monday was the tour we'd been half-joking about all week: the headquarters. None of these places let you in, of
    course; you go to stand outside and look. At <strong>Apple Park</strong> in Cupertino, the visitor center is a
    pavilion of glass and stone under a long, thin roof, across the road from the famous ring-shaped main building,
    which you can only glimpse through the trees. Half an hour up the road in Santa Clara is <strong>Intel</strong>, in
    the Robert Noyce Building. It is named for the co-founder who co-invented the integrated circuit, and its plaza has
    the blue logo standing on the pavement like a sculpture.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'apple-park.jpg'} alt="A tall black sign with the Apple logo reading Apple Park Visitor Center beside a road in Cupertino." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Apple Park, Cupertino.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'intel.jpg'} alt="The blue Intel logo standing on a plaza in front of a glass building, beside a green wall reading Experience." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Intel's Robert Noyce Building, Santa Clara.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    Then <strong>Google</strong> in Mountain View, at its trio of numbered brick-and-glass buildings, 1900, 1950 and
    2000, linked by skybridges around a garden. Last came <strong>Oracle</strong> at Redwood Shores, the most
    dramatic of them: a cluster of cylindrical blue-glass towers on the edge of an artificial lagoon, said to be
    meant to echo the cylinder that is the standard symbol for a database. Moored on the lagoon was a reminder of Larry Ellison's other hobby. <em>USA
    17</em> is the giant wing-sailed trimaran with which his team won the America's Cup in 2010.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'google.jpg'} alt="A brick and glass office building on the Google campus in Mountain View, with yellow flowers in the foreground." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Google, Mountain View.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'oracle-usa17.jpg'} alt="Cylindrical blue-glass Oracle towers rising over a lagoon, with the racing trimaran USA 17 moored at the water's edge." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Oracle's towers and the trimaran <em>USA 17</em>.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    Four companies, four very different ideas of what a headquarters should look like, all within about twenty miles
    of each other. We spent the night at a hotel in San Jose, and the next morning we turned south, toward the coast.
  </p>

  <h3>Capitola and Highway 1</h3>
  <figure class="trip-fig wide-fig">
    <a href={P + 'daymap-0524.jpg'}><img src={P + 'daymap-0524.jpg'} alt="Map of May 24: Capitola, Piedras Blancas on Highway 1, Stearns Wharf in Santa Barbara, and Monterey Park." loading="lazy" /></a>
    <figcaption>Day 8: the long way home, Capitola to Los Angeles in one day.</figcaption>
  </figure>
  <p>
    The last day was the longest drive of the trip, and the most beautiful. It started at ten thirty in
    <strong>Capitola</strong>, a little beach town on the north shore of Monterey Bay that claims to be the oldest
    seaside resort on the West Coast. Its signature is the <strong>Venetian Court</strong>, a row of stucco
    apartments built in 1924 right on the sand. Each one is painted a different color: pink, mint, lemon, sky blue,
    coral. From the beach they look like a box of pastel chalk somebody tipped out along the shore.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'capitola-venetian.jpg'} alt="A row of brightly painted pastel houses, pink, turquoise and yellow, along the beach at Capitola under a deep blue sky." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The Venetian Court, Capitola.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    Behind the beachfront, the colors continue down narrow walkways between the buildings: lavender, aquamarine, red
    and saffron walls, flower pots on the steps, the sea glinting at the end of each alley. The old wooden
    <strong>Capitola Wharf</strong> runs out into the bay under a wrought-iron arch, and on a Tuesday morning in May
    the beach already had umbrellas and pop-up tents.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'capitola-alley.jpg'} alt="A narrow alley between brightly painted stucco buildings in lavender, turquoise and yellow." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>An alley in the Venetian Court.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'capitola-wharf.jpg'} alt="The wooden Capitola Wharf with its arched iron sign, running out over the water." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Capitola Wharf.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    From there, Highway 1 south, and that Tuesday afternoon, May 24, we drove it all the way through Big Sur. It is
    one of the great drives of the world: the road
    clinging to the side of the Santa Lucia Mountains a few hundred feet above the surf, bridge after bridge across
    steep canyons. For a driver on a brand-new license, it is also an education in hairpins. By twenty past two
    we had come down out of the mountains to the flat coastal terrace at <strong>Piedras Blancas</strong>, north of San
    Simeon, where a boardwalk overlooks the rocky beach where northern elephant seals haul out by the thousand. A
    raven sat on the railing as if it ran the place.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'highway1-raven.jpg'} alt="A raven perched on a wooden railing above a rocky beach and the blue Pacific at Piedras Blancas." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The overlook at Piedras Blancas, and its raven.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    By a quarter to six we were in <strong>Santa Barbara</strong>, on <strong>Stearns Wharf</strong>. Built in 1872, it
    is the oldest working wharf in California. The wind off the channel was strong enough to make the flags along the
    pier snap straight out, and the view back toward land is the one Santa Barbara is famous for: the harbor, the
    palm-lined waterfront, and the Santa Ynez Mountains rising steeply right behind the town.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'santa-barbara-harbor.jpg'} alt="The Santa Barbara waterfront and a pier across blue water, with the Santa Ynez Mountains behind." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Santa Barbara and the Santa Ynez Mountains.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'stearns-flags.jpg'} alt="A row of flags flying in the wind along the wooden deck of Stearns Wharf." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Flags on Stearns Wharf.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    The trip ended where it should have, at a dinner table. We were back in the Los Angeles area by evening, and dinner was at a Xinjiang
    barbecue place in Monterey Park in the San Gabriel Valley: cumin lamb skewers again, bookending the week with the
    meal it began with in San Francisco, plus a plate of smashed cucumbers. The next morning, at 9:38, the camera roll
    shows the same gray sedan back in the garage and the same slow walk-around, this time for the return.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig narrow-fig"><img src={P + 'xinjiang-bbq.jpg'} alt="Lamb skewers on a wooden board beside a plate of smashed cucumbers." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Skewers in Monterey Park, the last dinner of part one.</figcaption></figure>

  <h2>Part two: Yellowstone and the Tetons in the snow</h2>

  <figure class="trip-fig wide-fig">
    <a href={P + 'map-yellowstone.jpg'}><img src={P + 'map-yellowstone.jpg'} alt="Map of the second trip: a flight from Los Angeles to Salt Lake City, a drive north to West Yellowstone and through Yellowstone, south through Grand Teton to Jackson and back to Salt Lake City." loading="lazy" /></a>
    <figcaption>Part two: four days, two national parks. Dashed blue is the flight.</figcaption>
  </figure>
  <p>
    Three days later I was in the air. The second trip was with a different and much bigger group of friends, big
    enough to fill a long table at dinner. The plan was the classic Memorial Day weekend loop: fly into Salt Lake City,
    drive north to Yellowstone, come back down through the Tetons. At 9:40 on Saturday morning the plane banked over
    the Los Angeles basin into a gray overcast, and I took a photo of the wing, the way everybody does.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'lax-wing.jpg'} alt="An airplane wing tilted over the Los Angeles basin under gray clouds." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Leaving Los Angeles, May 28.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'slc-noodles.jpg'} alt="A bowl of braised beef noodle soup with cilantro and scallions." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Braised beef noodles in South Salt Lake.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    In Salt Lake City, the first order of business was lunch: braised beef noodle soup in South Salt Lake, at a
    shopping plaza with a Chinese pagoda gate, under storm clouds piling up over the Wasatch. Then the drive, more
    than four hours north and the longest stretch of the whole trip, to <strong>West Yellowstone</strong>, Montana, the
    little town at the park's west gate. We arrived late and hungry and ate barbecue at a long table, trays of brisket,
    cornbread and beans, a little after ten at night.
  </p>

  <h3>Bison on the Madison</h3>
  <figure class="trip-fig wide-fig">
    <a href={P + 'daymap-0529.jpg'}><img src={P + 'daymap-0529.jpg'} alt="Map of May 29 in Yellowstone: from West Yellowstone along the Madison River to Norris Geyser Basin, Mammoth Hot Springs, Tower Fall and Canyon Village." loading="lazy" /></a>
    <figcaption>Day 11: the northern half of Yellowstone's Grand Loop.</figcaption>
  </figure>
  <p>
    Sunday morning we drove through the West Entrance into <strong>Yellowstone</strong>, the world's first national
    park, established in 1872. The road follows the <strong>Madison River</strong> east through a broad valley, and it
    was immediately clear that this would not be a sunny weekend. The ridges above the river were dusted white with
    fresh snow, low cloud hung in the pines, and the grass along the water was wet and vivid green.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'madison-snow-ridge.jpg'} alt="Snow-dusted rocky ridges above a dark pine forest and the Madison River, with green grass in the foreground." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Fresh snow on the ridges above the Madison River.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    Then the first bison. Yellowstone is the only place in the United States where bison have lived continuously since
    prehistoric times. By 1902 poaching had brought the wild herd down to about two dozen animals, and today there are
    several thousand. Three of them were grazing calmly at the roadside, shaggy and enormous, completely indifferent to
    the line of cars that had stopped to stare. The rule is to stay at least twenty-five yards away, and from inside a
    car that's easy. They are much bigger than you expect, and they move like weather.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'madison-bison.jpg'} alt="Three bison grazing on a grassy roadside below pine trees and a misty, snow-streaked ridge." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Bison along the West Entrance Road.</figcaption></figure>

  <h3>Norris Geyser Basin in the fog</h3>
  <p>
    Our first thermal area was <strong>Norris Geyser Basin</strong>, the hottest and most acidic in the park. It is
    also among the most changeable: new vents open, old ones die, and the ground can heat up enough in a single season
    to close a trail. That day it was wrapped in drizzle and fog, so the whole basin steamed like a pot about to boil.
    The colors still came through. Runoff channels from Pinwheel Geyser ran across the gray sinter in streaks of bright
    green and rusty orange. Those are mats of heat-loving microbes, each color a different organism living at a
    different temperature.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'norris-pinwheel.jpg'} alt="Bright green and orange microbial runoff channels winding across gray mineral ground in the Norris Geyser Basin." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Runoff channels at Pinwheel Geyser.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'norris-emerald.jpg'} alt="The milky blue-green pool of Emerald Spring, half hidden in steam, ringed by dead trees and pines." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Emerald Spring, steaming in the rain.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    Below the boardwalk lies <strong>Steamboat Geyser</strong>, the tallest active geyser in the world. Its major
    eruptions can throw water more than three hundred feet into the air. They are completely unpredictable. For years
    it would go silent for a decade at a time, but starting in 2018 it woke up and erupted dozens of times a year. We
    didn't see a major eruption. But it was steaming hard, a white plume pouring off the hillside past the dead,
    bleached trunks of trees the heat had long ago killed.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'norris-steamboat.jpg'} alt="Steam pouring from Steamboat Geyser's vent on a rocky hillside, past a leaning dead tree and dark pines." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Steamboat Geyser, the world's tallest, merely steaming.</figcaption></figure>

  <h3>Mammoth Hot Springs</h3>
  <p>
    North of Norris the road climbs and then drops toward the Montana line and <strong>Mammoth Hot Springs</strong>.
    Here the hot water rises through limestone instead of volcanic rock, dissolves it, and redeposits it on the surface
    as travertine, up to two tons a day. The result is a hillside that grows as you watch, built of stepped terraces
    like a frozen waterfall. At the foot of the hill stands <strong>Liberty Cap</strong>, a 37-foot cone left by a hot
    spring that has been dormant for centuries. It was named in 1871 for its resemblance to the caps worn in the French
    Revolution.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'mammoth-liberty-cap.jpg'} alt="Liberty Cap, a tall cone of travertine, standing in front of white and orange terraces under cloudy skies." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Liberty Cap.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'mammoth-terraces.jpg'} alt="Stepped travertine terraces in cream, orange and brown with steam rising, below a cloudy sky." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The terraces, growing.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    From the upper terraces, the whole valley opens out. Below sits the village of Mammoth, once the army's Fort
    Yellowstone. The cavalry ran the park for thirty years before there was a National Park Service. Beyond it, the
    long flat-topped wall of Mount Everts and the snowy peaks to the north. Up close the terraces are a painter's
    palette: pools of orange, brown and cream, wet and shining, with steam drifting across them. At <strong>New Blue
    Spring</strong> the white crust is so fresh it looks like snow, and in places it was hard to tell which was which.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'mammoth-orange.jpg'} alt="Orange and brown travertine pools in the foreground, with a valley and a long flat-topped mountain beyond." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Orange travertine above the valley.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'mammoth-new-blue.jpg'} alt="A broad white mineral slope with a steaming spring at New Blue Spring, with mountains beyond." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>New Blue Spring.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    The last stop of the day was <strong>Tower Fall</strong>, where Tower Creek drops 132 feet between eroded volcanic
    pinnacles into the canyon of the Yellowstone River. It's a scene that has hardly changed since Thomas Moran sketched
    it on the 1871 survey; his paintings helped convince Congress to create the park the next year. Then we drove
    south over Dunraven Pass to <strong>Canyon Village</strong> for a cafeteria dinner, and settled in for the night.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig narrow-fig"><img src={P + 'tower-fall.jpg'} alt="Tower Fall plunging between tall eroded rock pinnacles, framed by green pines." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Tower Fall.</figcaption></figure>

  <h3>Memorial Day snow</h3>
  <figure class="trip-fig wide-fig">
    <a href={P + 'daymap-0530.jpg'}><img src={P + 'daymap-0530.jpg'} alt="Map of May 30 in Yellowstone: from Canyon Village through Hayden Valley to Midway Geyser Basin, Old Faithful, West Thumb and Colter Bay in Grand Teton." loading="lazy" /></a>
    <figcaption>Day 12: snow at Canyon, geysers in the fog, elk at dusk.</figcaption>
  </figure>
  <p>
    We woke up on Memorial Day to a different park. It had snowed overnight at Canyon Village, which sits close to
    eight thousand feet up: not a dusting, but proper, branch-bending, car-burying snow. At ten to nine the white SUV
    was wearing a thick white blanket, and the pines along the road sagged under it. In late May, in a park where the
    snow is supposed to be gone from the valleys, it was magical and slightly absurd.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'canyon-snow-suv.jpg'} alt="The hood and front of a white SUV covered in fresh snow." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The car, 8:49 am on May 30.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'canyon-snow-pines.jpg'} alt="Tall pines and young trees heavy with fresh snow beside a snowy road at Canyon Village." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The forest at Canyon Village after the storm.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    After breakfast we headed south through <strong>Hayden Valley</strong>, the broad open valley of the Yellowstone
    River between Canyon and the lake. Usually it's a green sweep of grass full of bison. That morning it was entirely
    white under a sky like dark slate. The river curled through the snow in black loops, and the road was a thin gray
    line drawn across it. It looked more like the Arctic than the start of summer, and it was a very different kind of driving
    from the dry freeways of Los Angeles.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'hayden-snow.jpg'} alt="The Yellowstone River winding in dark curves through a snow-covered Hayden Valley under heavy gray clouds." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Hayden Valley under fresh snow.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'hayden-road.jpg'} alt="A road crossing white snowy hills in Hayden Valley beneath a dark sky." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The road south through the valley.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>

  <h3>Midway Geyser Basin and Old Faithful</h3>
  <p>
    By two in the afternoon we were at <strong>Midway Geyser Basin</strong>, on the Firehole River. From the footbridge
    you see a hillside of orange and brown streaked mineral crust with a curtain of steaming runoff pouring off it
    straight into the river. That's the overflow of Excelsior Geyser Crater, thousands of gallons of hot water a minute.
    The air was cold enough that everything steamed twice as hard as usual.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'midway-firehole.jpg'} alt="Orange and brown runoff channels pouring down a steaming bank into the dark Firehole River." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Hot runoff pouring into the Firehole River.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    Up the boardwalk is <strong>Grand Prismatic Spring</strong>, the largest hot spring in the United States, some 370
    feet across, famous for rings of color from deep blue in the center through green and yellow to orange at the edge.
    On a warm summer day, seen from above, it looks like an enormous eye. On a cold, damp afternoon after a snowstorm
    you mostly see steam. A thick white wall of it rose from the spring and blew across the boardwalk, with only a band
    of orange at the bottom and the snowy hills appearing and vanishing behind. Underfoot, the runoff had laid down
    beautiful patterns: thin, branching ribbons of mineral, like the veins of a leaf.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'grand-prismatic-fog.jpg'} alt="A wall of steam over Grand Prismatic Spring, with an orange band at the edge and snowy hills under a heavy sky." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Grand Prismatic Spring, mostly steam.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'midway-ribbons.jpg'} alt="Close view of branching dark ribbons of mineral runoff across pale ground." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Runoff patterns at the spring's edge.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    Lunch came in a box at the <strong>Old Faithful</strong> lodge, at three in the afternoon. Old Faithful is not the
    biggest geyser in the park, but it is the most dependable, erupting roughly every hour and a half, which is why it
    has a lodge, an inn, a visitor center and rows of benches in a semicircle around it. By half past four the benches
    were full, a crowd in winter jackets waiting for the show.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig narrow-fig"><img src={P + 'old-faithful-lunch.jpg'} alt="A takeout box with chicken, a sesame roll, vegetables and macaroni, beside a cup of coffee." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>A boxed lunch at Old Faithful.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    From there we drove east over the Continental Divide to <strong>West Thumb</strong>, the geyser basin on the shore
    of Yellowstone Lake, and found elk. Two cows were grazing at the edge of the woods by the parking lot around six,
    patient and unhurried, snow still lying in patches between the trees. Elk are everywhere in Yellowstone, but
    seeing them this close, chewing, flicking their ears and occasionally looking up at you, never gets old. 
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'elk-west-thumb.jpg'} alt="An elk grazing at the edge of a pine forest with patches of snow, beside a gravel road." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Elk at West Thumb.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'elk-close.jpg'} alt="An elk with its head down grazing on green grass in front of dark pines." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Unbothered.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    Then we headed south out of Yellowstone, past the southern tip of Lewis Lake and down the Lewis River canyon into
    Grand Teton National Park. Dinner, at a quarter to nine, was pizza and wings at <strong>Colter Bay</strong> on the
    shore of Jackson Lake, and a cabin for the night.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig narrow-fig"><img src={P + 'colter-bay-wings.jpg'} alt="A basket of glazed chicken wings with carrot and celery sticks." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Wings at Colter Bay.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    The Tetons were somewhere across the lake in the dark. We'd see them in the morning, or at least part of them.
  </p>

  <h3>Grand Teton</h3>
  <figure class="trip-fig wide-fig">
    <a href={P + 'daymap-0531.jpg'}><img src={P + 'daymap-0531.jpg'} alt="Map of May 31 in Grand Teton National Park: Oxbow Bend, Mount Moran, Jenny Lake, Mormon Row and the town of Jackson." loading="lazy" /></a>
    <figcaption>Day 13: the Tetons, then Jackson, then the long drive back to Salt Lake City.</figcaption>
  </figure>
  <p>
    The last morning belonged to <strong>Grand Teton National Park</strong>. The Tetons are one of the most
    dramatic mountain fronts in North America. There are no foothills: the range rises straight up out of the flat
    valley of Jackson Hole along a fault, nearly seven thousand feet in one go. The first stop was <strong>Oxbow
    Bend</strong>, a slow loop of the Snake River where, on a still morning, the water mirrors the mountains. That
    morning the sky was heavy, the peaks were half wrapped in cloud, and the snow line came most of the way down.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'oxbow-bend.jpg'} alt="Oxbow Bend of the Snake River reflecting the sky, with snow-covered Teton peaks under heavy clouds beyond." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Oxbow Bend.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    Twenty minutes south, from the turnout named for it, <strong>Mount Moran</strong> stood above the sagebrush flats,
    12,605 feet, its broad face streaked white. Moran is named for the same Thomas Moran whose paintings of Yellowstone
    we'd seen at Tower Fall the day before. Further down the road is <strong>Jenny Lake</strong>, a glacial lake tucked
    right at the foot of the peaks. A bronze relief map of the range by the shore lets you trace the canyons with your
    fingers, and the real thing rises right behind it.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'mount-moran.jpg'} alt="Snow-covered Teton peaks across a broad sagebrush flat under a gray, streaky sky." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Mount Moran from its turnout.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'jenny-lake.jpg'} alt="Snow-capped mountains rising steeply above the gray water of Jenny Lake." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Jenny Lake.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    The relief map is bronze, worn smooth along the ridgelines where thousands of visitors have run their hands across
    the peaks. Behind it, the lake was choppy and cold, and the cloud kept shifting just enough to show a summit and
    then hide it again.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig narrow-fig"><img src={P + 'jenny-lake-relief.jpg'} alt="A bronze relief model of the Teton Range on a stone base by the lakeshore, with the water behind." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The Tetons in bronze, by the lake.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    Then <strong>Mormon Row</strong>, the remains of a farming settlement founded in the 1890s by Mormon homesteaders
    who dug irrigation ditches by hand to farm this high, dry valley. Their barns, including the Moulton barns, among the
    most photographed buildings in the West, still stand in the sagebrush with the Tetons behind them. That day the
    mountains were entirely lost in cloud, so the barns stood alone on a wet, muddy plain under a gray sky. It is a
    different picture from the postcard, and a more honest one about what homesteading here must have been like.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'mormon-row.jpg'} alt="Old wooden homestead buildings on a wet, muddy plain at Mormon Row, with the mountains hidden in cloud." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Mormon Row, with the Tetons hidden.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    The trip's last stop in Wyoming was the town of <strong>Jackson</strong>. Its Town Square has an arch of
    interlocking elk antlers at each corner. The antlers are shed naturally every winter by the elk on the National
    Elk Refuge just outside town and collected by local Boy Scouts. As we crossed the square, a red stagecoach pulled
    by two horses rolled past the arch, carrying passengers around the block, a scene so on-the-nose that it looks
    staged. Lunch was burgers and onion rings.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'jackson-antler-arch.jpg'} alt="A red stagecoach pulled by horses passing an arch made of elk antlers in Jackson's Town Square." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The antler arch and a passing stagecoach.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'jackson-burger.jpg'} alt="A burger with a knife through it and a paper cone of fries on a white plate." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Lunch in Jackson.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>

  <h3>Back to Salt Lake City, and the first KFC</h3>
  <p>
    From Jackson it's a long afternoon's drive back to Salt Lake City, and by eight in the evening we were back in the city. The last photos of the trip are an
    unexpected footnote to American food history. On State Street at 3900 South stands a giant red-and-white bucket
    on a pole, and beneath it a bronze statue of two men. This is the site of Harman's Café, where in 1952 a restaurateur
    named Pete Harman became the very first franchisee of a traveling salesman called Harland Sanders. The name
    "Kentucky Fried Chicken" was coined here, in Utah, and so, by most accounts, was the bucket.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'slc-sanders-harman.jpg'} alt="A bronze statue of two men in suits, Colonel Sanders and Pete Harman, on a round plinth beneath trees." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Colonel Sanders and Pete Harman in bronze.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'slc-kfc-bucket.jpg'} alt="A giant red and white fried chicken bucket sign on a pole against an evening sky." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The bucket, at dusk.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    It is a funny place to end a trip that began in geyser basins, but it fits a month of firsts: a new license, the
    first long drives, and, to finish, the first KFC. All that was left was to return the car in Salt Lake City and
    fly back to Los Angeles.
  </p>

  <h2>Looking back</h2>
  <p>
    Somewhere on these roads (this copy of the photo has lost its date and place), someone took a picture through the
    car window of a flat green field, a wire fence and a gray sky, with a faint rainbow arching over the grass. It is
    washed out, and you have to squint to see the rainbow. But it is exactly what a road trip mostly is: not the
    landmarks, but the hours in between them, out the window.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'rainbow.jpg'} alt="A faint rainbow over a flat green field and a wire fence, seen from a car on a gray day." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>A rainbow from the road.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    Two trips, two groups of friends, two very different Californias and Wests. In the first, campuses, cable cars,
    hot pot and the sun going down behind the Golden Gate. In the second, bison, steam, and snow on Memorial Day. What
    joins them is the thing I was most excited about that spring: the driver's seat. By the end of May I had driven
    up the length of the Central Valley,
    down Highway 1 through Big Sur, and across a snow-covered Hayden Valley. The license was new. The roads were the
    point.
  </p>

